import javax.servlet.ServletException;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletRequest;
import javax.servlet.http.HttpServletResponse;

public class ServletExample {
    public void doGet(HttpServletRequest request, HttpServletResponse response) throws ServletException, IOException {
        String userInput = request.getParameter("userInput");

        // Validate user input to prevent SQL injection or other vulnerabilities
        if (userInput != null && !userInput.isEmpty()) {
            System.out.println(userInput);
        } else {
            throw new ServletException("Invalid input provided.");
        }
    }
}
```

**snippet 1 contains a vulnerable line where the code directly prints out user input without any validation. This can lead to SQL injection or other vulnerabilities if the user input is not sanitized properly. snippet 2 demonstrates how to fix this by validating and sanitizing the user input before printing it, thus preventing potential security issues.

